WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.877
A diagram that makes one point.

2
00:00:02.157 --> 00:00:07.469
Your report needs an architecture diagram, and reviewers will look at it before they read a word.

3
00:00:07.749 --> 00:00:14.277
Google’s Technical Writing Two course has a short unit on illustrations with advice that transfers directly.

4
00:00:14.557 --> 00:00:25.203
Write the caption first, stating the takeaway (“Every tool call passes through a permission check; only read tools run without confirmation”), and then draw the picture that supports it.

5
00:00:25.483 --> 00:00:34.400
Limit the information in a single drawing; the course’s rule of thumb is about what you could explain in one paragraph, or around five bullet points.

6
00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:41.912
And for a complex system, show an overview first, then separate drawings for the subsystems a reader needs to understand.

7
00:00:42.192 --> 00:00:53.712
For the capstone that usually means two diagrams: the reference-architecture overview, simplified to your actual components, and one focused diagram of whichever path carries your argument.

8
00:00:53.992 --> 00:01:03.827
That is often the agent loop with its limits and stop conditions, or the verification path that decides between answering, abstaining, and routing to a person.

9
00:01:04.107 --> 00:01:06.347
Label arrows with what flows along them.

10
00:01:06.627 --> 00:01:10.125
Remove every box a reader doesn’t need for the caption’s point.
